Good morning, Jules,

Executive Summary

This executive adverse news briefing covers 9 items relevant to UK banking an=
d financial entities during 12=E2=80=9319 October 2026: eight UK developments=
 and one non-UK cyber incident retained for third-party read-across. Regulato=
ry pressure dominated the period: the FCA confirmed that Jarvis Investment Ma=
nagement (14 October) and ePayments Systems (15 October) entered special admi=
nistration, and the Upper Tribunal listed lender challenges to the FCA motor =
finance redress scheme for 16=E2=80=9326 February 2027, prolonging redress un=
certainty for exposed lenders such as Aldermore, which FirstRand is selling a=
fter raising its UK motor finance provision to =C2=A3749 million. Financial c=
rime developments included three FCA/NCA arrests targeting a suspected inside=
r dealing and money laundering ring and guilty pleas in a =C2=A32 million inv=
estment fraud. Operational and cyber items comprised a compromise of an IT se=
rvice provider's online banking platform at Germany's abcbank and a NatWest p=
hishing warning.

1. Regulatory Enforcement & Legal Risks

Jarvis Investment Management & ePayments Systems: The FCA confirmed that Jarv=
is Investment Management Limited entered special administration on 14 October=
 2026 (special administrators: Adam Stephens and Philip Hemming, S&W Partners=
 LLP) and that ePayments Systems Limited entered special administration on 15=
 October 2026. The FCA notices do not state the causes. Jarvis had operated u=
nder an FCA voluntary requirement restricting its activities since September =
2022. The regime prioritises the return of client money, custody assets and c=
ustomer funds; the FSCS has published guidance for Jarvis clients.
Source: https://www.fca.org.uk/news/news-stories/jarvis-investment-management=
-limited-enters-special-administration
Source: https://www.fca.org.uk/news/news-stories/epayments-systems-limited-en=
ters-special-administration

UK Financial Sector Governance (FCA Multi-Firm Review): On 12 October 2026, t=
he FCA set out its expectations following a multi-firm review of governance a=
rrangements, focusing on effective board challenge, the recording of decision=
-making and clear oversight of executive actions. Multi-firm review findings =
are supervisory expectations rather than new rules, but are likely to inform =
future supervisory assessments.
Source: https://www.globalfinregblog.com/2026/10/fca-sets-out-expectations-on=
-firms-governance-arrangements/

UK Asset Management & Banking Wealth Units (FCA CP26/35): The FCA published C=
onsultation Paper CP26/35 on 8 October 2026 (legal commentary dated 16 Octobe=
r). It proposes that NURS funds investing at least 50% in inherently illiquid=
 assets offer no more than one dealing day per month, with redemption notice =
periods of at least 90 days. The proposals are not yet binding: the consultat=
ion closes on 11 December 2026, final rules are expected in 2027 and a transi=
tion period runs to 2029. Wealth and banking divisions distributing such fund=
s should assess product liquidity terms.
Source: https://www.simmons-simmons.com/en/publications/cmvakk46l00xoudt0lst0=
uji8/cp26-35-redemption-terms-for-funds-investing-in-illiquid-assets

Aldermore Bank Plc / FirstRand (UK Motor Finance Redress): On 13 October 2026=
, it was reported that the Upper Tribunal has listed hearings for 16=E2=80=93=
26 February 2027 on challenges to the FCA's motor finance redress scheme brou=
ght by Volkswagen Financial Services, Mercedes-Benz Financial Services and Cr=
=C3=A9dit Agricole Auto Finance, alongside a challenge by Consumer Voice. Ald=
ermore is not a named party, but the outcome will shape its redress exposure.=
 FirstRand is selling Aldermore and exiting the UK, citing the redress regime=
; its UK motor finance provision stood at =C2=A3749 million at 30 June 2026.
Source: https://www.cityam.com/motor-finance-legal-battle-set-for-february-20=
27/
Source: https://www.billionaires.africa/2026/09/10/mary-vilakazi-takes-firstr=
and-out-of-britain-after-548-million-hit/

2. Financial Crime & Compliance Risks

UK Banking Sector (Lloyds, Santander, NatWest, Nationwide, TSB): A ClearScore=
 review dated 16 October 2026 found that banks apply the optional excess of u=
p to =C2=A3100 unevenly under the APP fraud reimbursement regime in force sin=
ce 7 October 2024. Lloyds and Santander deduct a fixed =C2=A3100, NatWest sai=
d an excess could apply and first direct may deduct it, while Nationwide and =
TSB do not apply one.
Source: https://www.clearscore.com/learn/managing-money/bank-fraud-scam-exces=
s-charge

Financial Conduct Authority (FCA/NCA Operation): On 13 October 2026, the FCA =
and the National Crime Agency arrested three people in a joint operation agai=
nst a suspected organised criminal group involved in insider dealing and mone=
y laundering. More than 100 officers searched five homes in London and Manche=
ster.
Source: https://www.fca.org.uk/news/press-releases/three-arrests-major-fca-op=
eration-against-criminal-insider-dealing-ring

Financial Conduct Authority (FCA Prosecution): On 12 October 2026, the former=
 director and the business operations manager of Nexus Independent Financial =
Advisers and Nexus Investment Managers pleaded guilty at Portsmouth Crown Cou=
rt to defrauding four clients of =C2=A32 million between 2019 and 2023. Sente=
ncing is set for 10 December 2026.
Source: https://www.fca.org.uk/news/press-releases/two-plead-guilty-after-cli=
ents-lose-2m-fraud-case

3. Operational & Cybersecurity Risks

abcbank GmbH (Germany) =E2=80=93 non-UK, third-party read-across: A German IT=
 blog report dated 18 October 2026, based on customer correspondence, states =
that unauthorised parties accessed individual system components at the IT ser=
vice provider operating the bank's online banking, leaving customer login una=
vailable for about three days. The bank told customers that no accounts, paym=
ents or other systems were affected, but some customer data (e.g. mobile phon=
e numbers) was accessed and customers were asked to change passwords. No offi=
cial bank statement had been published and the report is unverified. Not to b=
e confused with ABC International Bank Plc (UK).
Source: https://borncity.com/blog/2026/10/18/cyberangriff-auf-abc-bank-connec=
twise-cloud-backup-kompromittierung/

National Westminster Bank Public Limited Company: On 17 October 2026, NatWest=
 warned customers about fraudulent phishing emails mimicking the bank's brand=
ing and asking recipients to review and update their personal details. No con=
firmed losses or breach of bank systems were reported.
Source: https://www.birminghammail.co.uk/news/cost-of-living/natwest-issues-w=
arning-over-email-34752474

Kind regards,
Tiago Vieira

